Q: 1 same object that can be one of multiple classes instance. C++ equivalente for a Python code with language-agnostic code style A: <p>The core problem is: C++ is statically typed, so a single variable can't hold &quot;either a class_1 or a class_2&quot; the way Python lets you. Historically the go-to solution was runtime polymorphism, meaning you define an abstract base class with virtual methods, make class_1 and class_2 inherit from it, allocate on the heap, and pass pointers around. That works but has real costs: you have to modify every class to inherit from the base, you lose value semantics (now you're dealing with pointers/arrows and ownership), and the design diverges heavily from the Python original. Since C++17, <code>std::variant</code> (<a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant</a>) provides a closed, value-type discriminated union, exactly what Python's <code>Union[class_1, class_2]</code> expresses. A variant lives on the stack, copies/moves like a normal value, and knows at runtime which type it currently holds. To call a method on whatever is inside, <code>std::visit</code> (<a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant/visit" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant/visit</a>) takes a callable, typically a generic lambda with <code>auto&amp;</code> parameter, and dispatches to the right type. The lambda <code>[](auto&amp; o) { return o.func_A(); }</code> is structurally identical to Python's duck typing: it says &quot;call func_A() on whatever you are,&quot; and the compiler verifies at compile time that every type in the variant actually has that method. No base class, no virtuals, no heap allocation, no arrows, no changes to existing class files. The trade-off: you can't write <code>obj_A.func_A()</code> directly on a variant the way Python lets you on a Union, so you need the <code>std::visit</code> wrapper. A small helper function like <code>call_func_A()</code> keeps this ergonomic at call sites. For the scope question, <code>std::variant</code> default-constructs to its first alternative, so you can declare it before an if-else block and reassign inside, same lifetime pattern as Python.</p>
<p>So my suggestion is to use <code>std::variant</code> with <code>std::visit</code>. No base class needed, no modification to existing class files, value semantics (dot syntax, no arrows).</p>
<p><strong>func3_file.h</strong></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;variant&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &quot;class_1.h&quot;
#include &quot;class_2.h&quot;

// Direct equivalent of Union[class_1, class_2]
using ObjA_t = std::variant&lt;class_1, class_2&gt;;

// Calls func_A() on whichever type obj_A actually holds
inline std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; call_func_A(ObjA_t&amp; obj_A) {
    return std::visit([](auto&amp; o) { return o.func_A(); }, obj_A);
}

inline void func_3(ObjA_t&amp; obj_A) {
    auto var_3 = call_func_A(obj_A);
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>main.cpp</strong></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &quot;func3_file.h&quot;

int main() {
    std::string input_i;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;write the class name to work with: &quot;;
    std::getline(std::cin, input_i);

    // Scope problem solved: variant default-constructs to first type,
    // then gets reassigned inside the if-else. Lives in outer scope.
    ObjA_t obj_A;

    if (input_i == &quot;class_1&quot;)
        obj_A = class_1{};
    else if (input_i == &quot;class_2&quot;)
        obj_A = class_2{};

    while (true) {
        std::string input_j;
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;write the input_j: &quot;;
        std::getline(std::cin, input_j);

        auto results = call_func_A(obj_A);
        if (std::ranges::find(results, input_j) != results.end())
            break;
    }

    if (true)
        func_3(obj_A);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Key points:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>std::variant&lt;class_1, class_2&gt;</code> = Python's <code>Union[class_1, class_2]</code>. Exact same concept.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>std::visit([](auto&amp; o) { return o.func_A(); }, v)</code> = duck-typed dispatch. The lambda with <code>auto&amp;</code> calls <code>.func_A()</code> on whatever type is inside. Compile-time checked -- if any variant alternative lacks <code>func_A()</code>, you get a compiler error.</p>
</li>
<li><p>No modification to <code>class_1</code> or <code>class_2</code> source files. No virtual, no base class.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Value semantics throughout. Dot syntax on <code>o</code> inside the lambda, no <code>-&gt;</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Adding <code>class_N</code>: update the <code>using ObjA_t</code> alias, add one more <code>else if</code> in <code>main</code>. That's it.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The one indirection vs Python: you can't write <code>obj_A.func_A()</code> directly on a variant. You need the <code>std::visit</code> + lambda wrapper. The <code>call_func_A</code> helper keeps call sites clean -- closest you get to Python's implicit duck typing.</p>
<p>Scope: <code>ObjA_t obj_A;</code> declared before the if-else block, default-constructs to the first type (<code>class_1</code>). Reassigned inside the branch. Available in the while loop and beyond. Same lifetime pattern as the Python version.</p>
